```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: red
REAL_USE_CASE: No demonstrated production need. The packet shows a hypothetical long-generation serving fleet (decode>>prefill) where a minority of requests suffer quant degradation, but the L1 evidence (Qwen2.5-1.5B NF4 vs fp16, 160 real prompts, H100) finds the pre-decode signal collapses to domain routing: within-domain prompt-perplexity AUC is 0.591 (arith) and 0.456 (longqa) — at chance — and the quant-sensitivity router (AUC 0.838) exceeds a generic difficulty router (AUC 0.813) by only +0.025, within noise. The only residual path named by the researcher (7B-AWQ + long-generation workloads) is untested.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only, not an operational win. Measured prompt:decode ratio is 178:71 tokens; prefill FLOPs (5.47e11) dominate decode (2.17e11 quant, 2.23e11 full). The router requires a quant prefill to extract prompt-ppl, costing 71.6% of a full quant forward pass. Rerouting a sensitive request incurs net +5.53e11 FLOPs because the skipped decode (2.17e11) is smaller than the prefill already paid. The “skip cheap decode” cost-win does not materialize. At true cost, the pre-decode router Pareto front overlaps a post-decode confidence-threshold cascade; at f>=0.6 the cascade reaches higher quality because it sees the real output. No strict Pareto dominance over (a) all-quant, (b) all-full, or (c) confidence routing is shown.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [prefill cost dominates: router is not free, 71.6% of forward pass paid before decision; signal is domain tag, not quant-specific — within-domain perplexity at chance; generic difficulty/domain router matches performance (+0.025 AUC); post-decode confidence cascade is competitive and uses strictly more information; NF4 (bitsandbytes) is not the production quant (AWQ/GPTQ) and 1.5B is not representative of fleet models; measured workload (178 tok prompt, 71 tok decode) does not reflect long-generation regime where cost story could survive; operational complexity of maintaining a pre-decode router vs. existing paged-attention kernels and confidence-based fallbacks]
RATIONALE: Packet L1 (EXP-0022, researcher-gpu-0022, verified RESULTS.md) reports real per-request degradation is heavy-tailed (objective domains n=120: mean delta 0.075, skew +0.39, excess kurtosis +3.28, Gini|delta| 0.833; 79.2% |delta|<0.05, 15% degrade >0.2) — the premise survives. However, the predictive claim fails: full-feature AUC 0.838 is carried by domain tag; within-domain ppl AUC 0.591 (arith), 0.456 (longqa). Quant-sensitivity vs generic-difficulty router delta is +0.025 AUC (0.838 vs 0.813). Cost accounting shows prefill 5.47e11 FLOPs = 71.6% of quant forward pass; reroute net cost +5.53e11 FLOPs. Confidence cascade AUC 0.695, Pareto overlap, neither dominates. The researcher’s honest residual names only untested 7B-AWQ + long-generation as revival path. The negative result is actionable: do not deploy pre-decode quant-sensitivity routers; use domain/difficulty routing or post-decode confidence thresholds instead. (v001)
```
